      "content": "I see. So the threshold for \"substantial interference\" is quite high, even if the pattern of disruptions is constant and highly specific. \n\nYou mentioned the difficulty of finding a \"goblin in the weeds\" of the employee situation—I'm curious if you're using that as a metaphor for how difficult it is to find an identifiable, malicious actor behind such seemingly irrational behavior? \n\nTo bring it back to the legal specifics, if the tenant can't identify a specific person, but the *pattern* of these \"petty\" disruptions is so pervasive and interconnected that it feels less like a series of accidents and more like a systemic, almost sentient presence of chaos, is there any legal doctrine that addresses this kind of \"unseen\" but pervasive disruption to the business? Or is the law strictly limited to what can be physically pinpointed and attributed to a specific entity?",
